EXT. DRY LAKE SALT WORKS - DAY

White. Everywhere white. A cracked lakebed baked flat. Heat shimmer swallows the horizon. A rusted rake the size of a gate. Mounded salt in rows like frozen waves. A tin-roofed shack leans against the wind that isn't there.

MORT GILCHRIST, 60, owner. Leathery. One suspender. Sits on an overturned bucket in the shack's only shade. Whittling nothing.

Beside him, ABEL STROUD, 17, his hired hand. Sunburned nose. Arms like fence rails. Sorting salt crystals into jars.

A cloud of dust on the horizon. Growing.

ABEL
Rider.

MORT
Two.

ABEL
How d'you know?

MORT
Dust's got two tails.

The dust resolves into two horsemen. Dark hats. Long coats in the heat. Both with rifles across their saddles.

TENNESSEE PELL, 40s, in front. Black beard, a gold tooth that catches the sun. Behind him, HOLCOMB VANCE, 30s, thin, quiet, scar down the throat.

They rein up at the shack. Dismount. Pell takes off his hat.

PELL
Water.

MORT
Barrel's by the door.

Pell dips a tin cup and drinks. Water dribbles down his chin into the salted dust. He hands the cup to Vance, who drinks without wiping it.

PELL
Heard you sell salt.

MORT
Heard right.

PELL
How much a sack?

MORT
Two dollars.

PELL
Last man charged me one.

MORT
Last man's dead.

Pell laughs. Vance does not.

PELL
He said you'd say that.

Abel's hand drifts toward a shovel resting against the wall. Mort pats the air, once, without looking.

MORT
Boy. Count the jars.

ABEL
I counted.

MORT
Count again.

Abel counts. His lips move. Pell watches him.

PELL
How old's the kid?

MORT
Old enough to count.

PELL
Is he yours?

MORT
He's his own.

PELL
Hm.

Pell takes a pinch of salt from a pile and tastes it. Spits.

PELL (CONT'D)
That's bitter.

MORT
It's lake salt. It's meant to be.

Vance wanders toward the rake. Runs a hand along the rusted tines. Looks at the rows of mounded white, then at Mort.

VANCE
Quiet out here.

MORT
That's the idea.

VANCE
How long you been alone?

MORT
Since the wife.

VANCE
He didn't say about a wife.

MORT
He wouldn't. He never met her.

A long silence. The heat ticks in the tin roof. A fly lands on the lip of the tin cup.

PELL
Two sacks.

MORT
Four dollars.

PELL
Three.

MORT
Four.

Pell reaches into his coat. Abel stiffens. Pell draws out, slowly, a leather purse. Counts four silver dollars onto the bucket lid.

MORT (CONT'D)
Boy. Load them.

Abel hefts two sacks onto the horses, one by one. Vance steadies the animals with a hand on each nose. Nobody says a word.

Pell mounts. Looks down at Mort.

PELL
You ever need a partner?

MORT
No.

PELL
Thought not.

He turns the horse. Vance follows. The dust takes them, a slow smear of two tails, then one, then none.

Abel exhales.

ABEL
Were they gonna?

MORT
Maybe.

ABEL
What stopped them?

MORT
The salt. Nobody robs a man with no water to steal.

He picks up his whittling again. Looks at it, shakes his head. Tosses the stick onto the salt.
